# ==============================================================================
# Halisaha — environment template for the Next.js app (apps/web)
#
#   cp .env.example apps/web/.env.local
#
# The copy goes in apps/web, not here. `npm run dev` runs `next dev` with apps/web
# as its working directory, and Next loads .env files relative to that directory —
# a copy left at the repository root is never read.
#
# NEVER commit the filled-in file: `.gitignore` allows only this `.env.example`.
#
# Naming rule: anything prefixed NEXT_PUBLIC_ is inlined into the JavaScript bundle
# and is therefore PUBLIC. Every other variable must stay server-side. Importing a
# module that reads one into a Client Component leaks it; the build does not fail.
#
# The Expo client and the Python sidecar have their own templates:
#   apps/mobile/.env.example        services/anomaly/.env.example
# Supabase Edge Functions read their own copies, set with `supabase secrets set`.
# ==============================================================================
#
# VALIDATED AT SERVER START. apps/web/lib/env.ts holds one zod schema for everything
# below, and apps/web/instrumentation.ts runs it once per server process BEFORE the
# first request. A deploy missing a required variable answers 500 to everything
# rather than starting, taking a booking, and failing when Stripe posts the first
# webhook.
#
# Note it is a RUNTIME gate: `next build` does not run the hook, so a broken
# environment still builds. See docs/PRODUCTION.md section 1a, which also explains
# why the deployment's health check has to be an HTTP one rather than a TCP one.
#
# It also enforces rules that span two variables, each of which prevents a specific
# production failure. The ones worth knowing before you edit anything here:
#
#   * STRIPE_SECRET_KEY without STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET is refused. That configuration
#     charges the customer and then drops payment_intent.succeeded, so the booking
#     never leaves `pending`: money in, no booking.
#   * STRIPE_CONNECT_WEBHOOK_SECRET must differ from STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET.
#   * Both Stripe keys must be the same mode, and neither may be a test key in a
#     production build.
#   * ANOMALY_SERVICE_URL and ANOMALY_SERVICE_SECRET: both or neither.
#   * In production: NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL (https), INTERNAL_API_TOKEN and
#     RESEND_API_KEY are all required. See docs/PRODUCTION.md section 1a for what
#     each one breaks when it is missing.
#
# Errors list every problem at once and never echo a value.
# ==============================================================================

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Supabase — project API
#    Dashboard > Project Settings > API. The anon key is safe in the browser
#    because Row Level Security is enabled on all 21 tables in `public`; 0002_rls.sql
#    writes the policies for the 19 created in 0001, and 0004/0005 cover the two
#    they add.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Read by lib/supabase/{client,server,middleware,admin}.ts, and by next.config.mjs
# to scope the Supabase Storage host in `images.remotePatterns`.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co

# Read by lib/supabase/{client,server,middleware}.ts. Every query it makes is
# still filtered by RLS.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.your-anon-key

# service_role BYPASSES RLS entirely. Read by lib/supabase/admin.ts only, for webhook
# handlers, connected-account id writes, and GDPR erasure. If this leaks, rotate it in
# the dashboard immediately — it is a full database key.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.your-service-role-key

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Supabase — Postgres connections (Supavisor pooler)
#
#    Neither string is read by the Next.js app: it talks to PostgREST over HTTPS
#    with the keys above. These are for tools that open a real connection.
#
#    Host shape: aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com ; user shape: postgres.<project-ref>
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# TRANSACTION mode, port 6543. The string to hand any serverless or edge runtime that
# connects directly: a connection is checked out per invocation and returned at COMMIT.
# `?pgbouncer=true` disables prepared-statement caching, which transaction pooling
# cannot support.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.your-project-ref:your-db-password@aws-0-eu-central-1.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres?pgbouncer=true&connection_limit=1

# SESSION mode, port 5432. For migrations, `supabase db push`, pg_cron, psql, and
# anything needing advisory locks, LISTEN/NOTIFY, server-side cursors or temp tables —
# none of which survive a transaction-pooled connection. services/anomaly/train.py
# falls back to this when ANOMALY_TRAINING_DATABASE_URL is unset, and refuses a :6543 URL.
DIRECT_URL=postgresql://postgres.your-project-ref:your-db-password@aws-0-eu-central-1.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Stripe Connect (Express, destination charges)
#    Use sk_test_/pk_test_ keys against a test-mode platform account until launch.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Read by lib/stripe.ts to construct the Stripe client. `_live_` in the value also
# switches the module's live-mode guards on.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_replace_me

# Read by app/(app)/checkout/[bookingId]/page.tsx and returned to the mobile client
# by POST /api/bookings/checkout.
NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=pk_test_replace_me

# Signing secret for the PLATFORM webhook endpoint (payment_intent.*, charge.*,
# checkout.session.*). Read by app/api/stripe/webhook/route.ts. Locally,
# `npm run stripe:listen` prints a whsec_... to paste here.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_replace_me

# Signing secret for the CONNECT webhook endpoint (account.updated, payout.*,
# capability.updated). Read by the same route, which tries both secrets in turn.
# Stripe issues a DIFFERENT secret for the connect endpoint — reusing the platform
# secret makes every connect event fail signature verification.
STRIPE_CONNECT_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_connect_replace_me

# Platform commission in BASIS POINTS of the booking subtotal (1000 = 10.00%).
# Read by lib/stripe.ts; becomes Stripe's `application_fee_amount` on the destination
# charge. Must be an integer in [0, 10000] — a value outside that throws at import
# rather than silently becoming a 0% or 100% take rate.
PLATFORM_FEE_BPS=1000

# Optional floor and ceiling on that fee, in minor units. Read by lib/stripe.ts.
# Defaults: 0 (no floor) and effectively unbounded.
# PLATFORM_FEE_MIN_MINOR=0
# PLATFORM_FEE_MAX_MINOR=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Booking rules
#    All optional; the defaults below are what the code uses when they are unset.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# How long an unpaid reservation holds its slot. Read by lib/booking/availability.ts
# (the countdown the customer sees) and app/api/internal/bookings/expire-reservations
# (the sweeper that releases it). Clamped to [10, 1440] in both places so they agree.
# BOOKING_RESERVATION_TTL_MINUTES=30

# Hours before kickoff that a cancellation still earns a full refund. Read by
# lib/payments.ts.
# BOOKING_CANCELLATION_WINDOW_HOURS=24

# Basis points of total_minor refunded for a cancellation INSIDE that window. Read by
# lib/payments.ts and clamped to [0, 10000]. 0 = no refund once the window closes.
# BOOKING_LATE_CANCELLATION_REFUND_BPS=5000

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. Anomaly detection sidecar (services/anomaly — FastAPI + IsolationForest)
#    Both are read by app/api/internal/anomaly/check/route.ts. With either unset the
#    route logs and scores with the in-database rule engine instead.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ANOMALY_SERVICE_URL=http://localhost:8000

# Shared HMAC key. Must be byte-identical to ANOMALY_SERVICE_SECRET in
# services/anomaly/.env, or every request comes back 401 signature_invalid.
# At least 32 characters. Generate: `openssl rand -hex 32`.
ANOMALY_SERVICE_SECRET=replace-with-32-byte-hex

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. Internal machine-to-machine calls
#    Guards /api/internal/* against the public internet. Presented as a bearer token
#    or an X-Internal-Token header by the Supabase Edge Functions, by pg_cron, and by
#    app/api/matches/[id]/report-score when it fires the anomaly check. Unset means
#    those routes answer 503 to everyone. Generate: `openssl rand -hex 32`.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
INTERNAL_API_TOKEN=replace-with-32-byte-hex

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. Transactional email (GDPR Art. 8 parental-consent links)
#    Transport selection lives in lib/gdpr.ts: RESEND_API_KEY wins, then SMTP_URL,
#    then the development console.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Resend API key. With none set outside production the consent link is printed to the
# server console; in production it is withheld from the log and the send reports failure.
RESEND_API_KEY=re_replace_me

# From address, first match wins. Falls back to "Halisaha <onboarding@resend.dev>".
RESEND_FROM_EMAIL=Halisaha <no-reply@example.com>
EMAIL_FROM=Halisaha <no-reply@example.com>

# Leave this EMPTY. Setting it selects an SMTP transport that is a stub: no SMTP client
# is bundled, so every consent email returns delivered:false with an explanation. It is
# a placeholder for a nodemailer implementation in lib/gdpr.ts, not a working fallback.
SMTP_URL=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8. Site origin
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Absolute base for Stripe return_url/refresh_url, Supabase auth redirects, the
# metadataBase in app/layout.tsx, and parental-consent links. No trailing slash. Read in
# preference to the request's Host header, deliberately — building a Stripe return_url
# from a header is host-header injection. In production set the real domain; Stripe
# rejects a localhost return_url on a live account.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Set by Vercel, not by you. lib/stripe.ts uses it as `https://$VERCEL_URL` when
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL is unset. Outside production the fallback is http://localhost:3000;
# in production with neither set, resolveSiteOrigin() throws.
# VERCEL_URL=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9. CORS for the Expo client
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Comma-separated bare origins allowed to call /api/* cross-origin, read by
# apps/web/next.config.mjs. Native React Native sends no Origin and is not subject to
# CORS; this is for Expo Web and browser-hosted builds. Entries with a trailing slash,
# a path, credentials or a custom scheme (halisaha://) are rejected at config load with
# a warning. Unset outside production falls back to the Expo dev ports (8081, 19006);
# unset in a production build allows nothing.
# MOBILE_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:8081,https://app.halisaha.example

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 10. Build dashboard (scripts/progress.mjs)
#     `node scripts/progress.mjs` reads the process environment directly and loads no
#     .env file, so these two have to be exported in the shell rather than set here.
#     Listed for completeness.
#
#     PORT                 — the dashboard's own port. Default 4321.
#     HALISAHA_WORKFLOWS   — directory holding the agent workflow journals it reads.
#                            Defaults to a path under $USERPROFILE / $HOME.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
